      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The stopping (retarding) potential Vₛ satisfies e·Vₛ = Kₘₐₓ (ignoring extraneous contact potentials).",
      "evidence": "By definition, Vₛ is the voltage that just reduces the photocurrent to zero, i.e., it must remove the kinetic energy of the most energetic electrons; in an ideal photo‑electric tube the only energy to be removed is Kₘₐₓ.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Therefore Vₛ = (h/e)·ν – φ/e, a linear function of the incident frequency.",
      "evidence": "Algebraic rearrangement of step 2 using the expression from step 1.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The linear relation holds only for frequencies above the threshold ν₀ = φ/h; below ν₀ no electrons are emitted and Vₛ is undefined.",
      "evidence": "Condition hf > φ is required for electron emission; this is the experimentally observed cutoff frequency.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Option D states exactly this: the stopping potential is a linear function of frequency above the threshold frequency.",
      "evidence": "Option D wording matches the conclusion of steps 3 and 4.",
